{
  "abstract": "Miranda, Movasati, Rufino and Villaflor [1] state the Hodge conjecture for all Fermat varieties of degree $d<65$ except $d\\in\\{44,51,52\\}$. Bhattacharjee [B] (unrefereed) gives a proof for $44,51,52$ through threefold characters whose quotient threefolds are uniruled. We give an independent proof by a different route. rStation paper 9 showed that for these $d$ the conjecture is equivalent to the algebraicity of one Galois orbit of Hodge classes on a product of four curves $D_1\\times\\dots\\times D_4$, $D_i:y^d=t^{a_i}(t-1)^{b_i}$. We prove that for $d=44,51$, and for $d=52$ with a different Hodge four-tensor in the same class, a resolution of the quotient $Y=(D_1\\times\\dots\\times D_4)/A$, $A=\\ker(\\mu_d^4\\to\\mu_d)$, is uniruled. The Hodge conjecture for uniruled fourfolds (Conte–Murre) then gives the Hodge conjecture for all Fermat and weighted Fermat varieties of degree 44, 51 and 52. The proof is a curve-side computation. Every pluricanonical form we computed on $Y$ vanishes, which by itself proves nothing. Instead we bound the canonical degree of lifted curves: for $\\varphi:\\mathbb P^1\\to(\\mathbb P^1)^4$ of total degree $D$, $K\\cdot\\Gamma'\\le dD-\\sum_x\\gcd(W_x,d)$, where $W_x$ is the weight of $\\varphi^*F$ at $x$ and $F$ is the branch function of $Y\\to(\\mathbb P^1)^4$. A linear program over coincidence patterns that can be imposed freely gives explicit dominant families with $K\\cdot\\Gamma'\\le-44$ (degree $(12,2,12,11)$), $K\\cdot\\Gamma'\\le-51$ (degree $(12,3,24,3)$) and, for $d=52$, $K\\cdot\\Gamma'\\le-48$ (degree $(9,24,9,24)$). For the tensor of [4] at $d=52$ no freely realizable pattern exceeds the bound (best ratio $0.9953$); a search over all 193 Hodge four-tensors with detector value 1 finds one that does. We check every certificate exactly over $\\mathbb Q$ and by an independent numerical construction.",
